Bootstrap confidence interval for loss ratio by policy type
A point estimate of loss ratio does not show uncertainty. The code chunk below uses bootstrap resampling to estimate confidence intervals for loss ratio by policy type.

GraphCode Chunk







Bootstrap confidence intervals for policy type loss ratios







set.seed(608)

bootstrap_loss_ratio &lt;- function(data, group_var, n_boot = 300) {
  group_var &lt;- enquo(group_var)

  data |&gt;
    filter(total_premium &gt; 0) |&gt;
    group_by(!!group_var) |&gt;
    group_modify(~{
      map_dfr(seq_len(n_boot), function(i) {
        sample_data &lt;- slice_sample(.x, n = nrow(.x), replace = TRUE)
        data.frame(
          boot_id = i,
          loss_ratio = sum(sample_data$total_incurred, na.rm = TRUE) /
            sum(sample_data$total_premium, na.rm = TRUE)
        )
      })
    }) |&gt;
    summarise(
      lower = quantile(loss_ratio, 0.025, na.rm = TRUE),
      median = median(loss_ratio, na.rm = TRUE),
      upper = quantile(loss_ratio, 0.975, na.rm = TRUE),
      .groups = "drop"
    )
}

policy_type_ci &lt;- bootstrap_loss_ratio(motor, policy_type, n_boot = 300) |&gt;
  mutate(policy_type = fct_reorder(policy_type, median))

ggplot(policy_type_ci,
       aes(x = median, y = policy_type)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = overall_loss_ratio, linetype = "dotted", colour = "grey35") +
  geom_errorbarh(aes(xmin = lower, xmax = upper), height = 0.15) +
  geom_point(size = 2.8) +
  scale_x_continuous(labels = percent) +
  labs(
    title = wrap_title("Uncertainty-aware comparison of policy type loss ratios"),
    subtitle = wrap_title("Intervals are based on bootstrap resampling within each policy type."),
    x = "Loss ratio",
    y = "Policy type"
  )




Insight

COMP_N has the highest estimated loss ratio, and its confidence interval remains well above most other policy types.
TP has the lowest estimated loss ratio, although its interval is wider because it is a smaller segment.
TPG has a wider interval than CC and COMP_E, suggesting more uncertainty in its estimated profitability.
This graph strengthens the analysis because it avoids relying only on point estimates.

Preparing the organisation chart
The organisation chart gives context to the people involved in the event chain. It is especially important because John Windward is not a generic user in the organisation. He is a department lead, and this makes the event more serious from a governance perspective.

org_nodes &lt;- purrr::map_dfr(org_raw$nodes, ~as_tibble(.x)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    title = title %||% NA_character_,
    label_clean = clean_actor_label(id)
  )

org_edges &lt;- purrr::map_dfr(org_raw$edges, ~as_tibble(.x))

person_parent &lt;- org_edges |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(target, "^person:")) |&gt;
  transmute(person_id = target, parent_id = source, relation)

team_department &lt;- org_edges |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(source, "^department:"), str_detect(target, "^team:")) |&gt;
  transmute(team_id = target, department_id = source)

department_labels &lt;- org_nodes |&gt;
  filter(type == "department") |&gt;
  transmute(department_id = id, department = label)

person_org &lt;- person_parent |&gt;
  left_join(team_department, by = c("parent_id" = "team_id")) |&gt;
  mutate(
    department_id = if_else(str_detect(parent_id, "^department:"), parent_id, department_id)
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(department_labels, by = "department_id") |&gt;
  left_join(org_nodes |&gt; select(person_id = id, person_name = label, title), by = "person_id")

john_org &lt;- person_org |&gt;
  filter(person_id == "person:john_windward") |&gt;
  select(person_name, title, department)

